.TH xrdmoncollect 8 "@XRootD_VERSION_STRING@"
.SH NAME
xrdmoncollect - collect and correlate XRootD detailed monitoring streams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf

\fBxrdmoncollect\fR [\fB-c\fR \fIfile\fR] \fB-p\fR \fIport\fR [\fB-b\fR \fIbindaddr\fR] [\fB-o\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB--bulk\fR \fIindex\fR]
              [\fB--os-url\fR \fIurl\fR [\fB--os-index\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB--os-user\fR \fIuser\fR]
               [\fB--os-pass\fR \fIpass\fR] [\fB--os-token\fR \fItoken\fR] [\fB--os-insecure\fR] [\fB--os-datastream\fR]]
              [\fB--otlp-url\fR \fIurl\fR [\fB--otlp-token\fR \fItoken\fR] [\fB--otlp-insecure\fR]]
              [\fB--cache-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
              [\fB--forward\fR \fIhost:port\fR] [\fB--metrics-port\fR \fIport\fR] [\fB--site\fR \fIname\fR]
              [\fB--tcp-port\fR \fIport\fR [\fB--tcp-token\fR \fItoken\fR]]
              [\fB--shovel\fR \fIhost:port\fR [\fB--shovel-token\fR \fItoken\fR] [\fB--spool-max\fR \fIsz\fR]]
              [\fB--flush-count\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB--flush-secs\fR \fIn\fR]
              [\fB--rcvbuf\fR \fIsz\fR] [\fB--queue-depth\fR \fIn\fR]
              [\fB--max-memory\fR \fIsz\fR] [\fB--max-entries\fR \fIn\fR]
              [\fB--server-ttl\fR \fIs\fR] [\fB--file-ttl\fR \fIs\fR]
              [\fB--state-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB--state-ttl\fR \fIdur\fR]
              [\fB--scitags\fR \fIsrc\fR [\fB--scitags-refresh\fR \fIs\fR]] [\fB--dataset\fR \fIre\fR]
              [\fB--no-resolve\fR]
              [\fB--sessions\fR] [\fB--spans\fR] [\fB--traces\fR] [\fB--gstream\fR] [\fB--redirects\fR] [\fB--debug\fR] [\fB-v\fR]

\fBxrdmoncollect\fR \fB-h\fR | \fB--help\fR

.fi
.br
.ad l
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBxrdmoncollect\fR daemon receives the binary UDP \fIdetailed monitoring\fR
streams emitted by one or more \fBxrootd\fR servers (the \fIu\fR, \fId\fR,
\fIi\fR, \fI=\fR, \fIf\fR, \fIt\fR, \fIg\fR, \fIr\fR, \fIT\fR, \fIU\fR, and FRM
\fIx\fR/\fIp\fR streams configured via the \fBxrootd.monitor\fR directive) and
correlates them into self-contained JSON documents.
.P
The principal output is one document per file close, joining the
\fIf\fR-stream close record with the user, path, application-info, token, and
activity dictionaries into a single record carrying WLCG-aligned identity,
location, and transfer fields. Documents use an OpenTelemetry-aligned schema: a
process-level \fBresource\fR object and an event-level \fBattributes\fR object
keyed by dotted semantic-convention names (with XRootD-specific fields under the
\fBxrootd.*\fR vendor namespace; for example \fBresource.server.address\fR,
\fBattributes.client.address\fR, \fBattributes.xrootd.vo\fR,
\fBattributes.xrootd.read_bytes\fR).
A store that expands dotted field names (such as OpenSearch) indexes each key as
a nested field, and empty or zero-valued fields are omitted. The OpenSearch
\fB_bulk\fR and OTLP sinks serialize this one canonical document, so they carry
the same fields and differ only in wire encoding (nested JSON vs typed
\fBKeyValue\fR arrays); see the README for a field-by-field sink comparison and
the per-stream wire-field-to-JSON mapping tables. There is no
top-level \fBtype\fR field: the record kind is the top-level \fBeventName\fR
(the OTLP LogRecord EventName field), duplicated as
\fBattributes["event.name"]\fR for stores that only query attributes (such as
Loki structured metadata).
.P
The event names the operation. A file close is \fBxrootd.read\fR or
\fBxrootd.write\fR depending on whether it carried write bytes; an operation
that failed without producing a close is \fBxrootd.\fIcategory\fR
(\fBxrootd.open\fR, \fBxrootd.auth\fR and so on); a redirect is
\fBxrootd.redirect\fR. Per-I/O trace records (\fB--traces\fR) are named
\fBxrootd.io.*\fR, keeping the much higher volume detail stream apart from the
per-file documents. Whether a close moved the file in its entirety is left to
the consumer, which has \fBfile.size\fR and the byte counters
(\fBxrootd.read_bytes\fR, \fBxrootd.readv_bytes\fR, \fBxrootd.write_bytes\fR)
in the same document; the collector reports what the server measured and derives
nothing from it. Operations are counted in
\fBxrootd_collector_io_total{cluster,server,operation}\fR, whose \fBoperation\fR label
is \fBopen\fR, \fBclose\fR, or -- when the server config includes the \fBops\fR
block -- \fBread\fR, \fBreadv\fR and \fBwrite\fR per request.
.P
A transfer document also carries the terminal status of the operation in
\fBxrootd.operation.state\fR: a normal close reports \fBSuccessful\fR, while a
failed open, a mid-transfer read/write error, or a failed close reports
\fBFailed\fR together with \fBxrootd.operation.name\fR
(\fBopen\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBwrite\fR, \fBclose\fR, or \fBauth\fR),
\fBxrootd.error.code\fR, and the server's error message in
\fBerror.type\fR. Failed opens, which
produce no close record, are reported by the server as a dedicated terminal
record on the \fIf\fR-stream; mid-transfer and close failures travel with the
close record itself. A redirect (\fIr\fR-stream, with \fB--redirects\fR) is a
third terminal state, \fBRedirected\fR: from the redirector's point of view the
operation concluded by sending the client elsewhere, with the destination in the
\fBxrootd.redirect.*\fR attributes.
.P
With \fB--sessions\fR, a \fIsession\fR document
(\fBattributes["event.name"]\fR = \fBxrootd.session\fR) aggregates each
disconnecting client's whole session (off by default; when disabled no session
rollup is kept and no session document is emitted):
every file close that named the user is folded into running totals
(\fBxrootd.session.files\fR, \fB.reads\fR, \fB.writes\fR,
\fB.read_bytes\fR, \fB.readv_bytes\fR, \fB.write_bytes\fR, \fB.errors\fR, and
start/end/duration) plus a capped \fBxrootd.session.recent_files\fR list (each
with \fBfile.path\fR, \fBxrootd.operation.name\fR, and
\fBxrootd.bytes\fR). The totals cover every closed file; only the recent-file
list is bounded, so a long session that opens many files stays memory-bounded.
.P
\fBxrootd.session.start_time\fR, \fB.end_time\fR and \fB.duration\fR are always
present on a session document, and the duration is never negative. The window
also encloses every record the session accounts for, so no open, I/O trace,
close, error or redirect belonging to it falls outside and, with \fB--spans\fR,
no child span escapes its parent. This needs stating because no record carries
an exact time: a record's time is interpolated across the window of the packet
carrying it, and two packets \(em especially from two streams, which window
independently \(em carry independent estimation error, so a transfer that
finished before the client disconnected can arrive stamped after it. The
earliest and latest time of any record naming a session are tracked and the
resolved window widened to cover both.
.P
The same holds one level down, for the \fB--spans\fR waterfall
session \(-> file \(-> I/O. A trace-stream I/O record is the coarsest thing the
collector handles \(em records after the last window mark all land on that
window's start \(em so it is clamped into the interval its file is known to have
been open (it cannot precede the open, nor follow the moment the packet
reporting it arrived here), and whatever still outruns the close widens the
file's span rather than the record being left hanging outside it. The clamp does
not touch \fBxrootd.operation.duration\fR, which stays the open \(-> close
estimate. An I/O record that arrives before the open of its file cannot be
correlated at all; see \fBSpan nesting\fR in README.md for what that costs.
.P
The start has a further problem: no dictionary record carries a time on the wire
at all, so the login can only be timed by the collector, whose clock is not the
reporting server's; the offset between the two is estimated per server
incarnation from the window ends the \fBf\fR stream carries.
\fBxrootd.session.start_time_source\fR reports how the start was arrived at, in
decreasing authority: \fBlogin\fR (the connect duration the \fBt\fR-stream
disconnect carries), \fBconnect\fR (the login record's arrival, translated into
the server's clock), \fBfirst_activity\fR (the earliest record naming the
session) or \fBdisconnect\fR (nothing else was available, so the session is
reported as an instant). The earliest admissible candidate wins, ties going to
the more authoritative one, so \fBfirst_activity\fR also covers activity that
predates a login estimate. Candidates are accepted only within the session's own
bounds, so a skewed clock degrades the start to a later source rather than
producing a wrong or missing one. The counter
\fBxrootd_collector_session_starts_total{cluster,server,source}\fR shows the mix: mostly
\fBdisconnect\fR means \fBu\fR records are being lost, or the server's
\fBxrootd.monitor\fR destination lacks the \fBuser\fR flag.
.P
Documents are emitted as newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) to a file or standard
output, posted directly to an OpenSearch cluster, exported as OTLP/JSON to an
OpenTelemetry collector (\fB--otlp-url\fR), and/or streamed over TCP to a
buffering frontend. The collector can additionally serve aggregated Prometheus
metrics over HTTP.
.P
For large or distributed clusters, where long-haul UDP to a central collector
is lossy, \fBxrdmoncollect\fR can also run as a \fIshoveler\fR: started with
\fB--shovel\fR \fIhost:port\fR next to each daemon, it does not decode at all
but encapsulates every received UDP datagram (together with its original source
address) in a simple length-prefixed TCP framing and relays it to a central
collector listening with \fB--tcp-port\fR, which re-injects the datagrams into
its normal pipeline attributed to the original sender. The channel carries an
optional shared-secret token (\fB--shovel-token\fR / \fB--tcp-token\fR), and the
shoveler spools frames to disk under \fB--cache-dir\fR while the collector is
unreachable, replaying them oldest-first on reconnect (capped by
\fB--spool-max\fR, evicting the oldest buffers). See the README for the frame
layout and deployment guidance.
.P
Usage synopsis can be displayed by typing "\fBxrdmoncollect --help\fR".
.SH OPTIONS
\fB-c\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB--config\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 5
Load options from an INI configuration file. Keys live in a single
\fB[xrdmoncollect]\fR section and mirror the long-option names; command-line
options override values from the file. When \fB-c\fR is not given, the default
file \fI/etc/xrootd/xrdmoncollect.cfg\fR is loaded if it exists (a missing
default is silently ignored). A named-but-unreadable or malformed file is a
fatal error. See the \fBCONFIGURATION FILE\fR section.
.RE
\fB-p\fR \fIport\fR, \fB--udp-port\fR \fIport\fR
.RS 5
UDP port to listen on (required unless \fB--tcp-port\fR is given).
.RE
\fB-b\fR \fIbindaddr\fR
.RS 5
Address to bind. The default binds all interfaces using an IPv6 dual-stack
socket.
.RE
\fB-o\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 5
Append output to \fIfile\fR. The default is standard output unless a network
sink (\fB--os-url\fR or \fB--forward\fR) is configured.
.RE
\fB--bulk\fR \fIindex\fR
.RS 5
Write the file/stdout sink in OpenSearch \fB_bulk\fR format using \fIindex\fR.
.RE
\fB--os-url\fR \fIurl\fR
.RS 5
POST documents to an OpenSearch cluster's \fB_bulk\fR API at \fIurl\fR. Requires
that \fBxrdmoncollect\fR was built with libcurl. This defines the destination
named \fBdefault\fR; further destinations are declared in the configuration file
(see \fBMULTIPLE DESTINATIONS\fR).
.RE
\fB--os-index\fR \fIname\fR
.RS 5
Index or data-stream name (default: \fBxrootd-transfers\fR).
.RE
\fB--os-user\fR \fIuser\fR, \fB--os-pass\fR \fIpass\fR
.RS 5
HTTP basic-auth credentials for the OpenSearch endpoint.
.RE
\fB--os-token\fR \fItoken\fR
.RS 5
Bearer token for the OpenSearch endpoint, sent as an
\fBAuthorization: Bearer \fR\fItoken\fR header. Takes precedence over basic auth
(\fB--os-user\fR/\fB--os-pass\fR) when both are given. If \fItoken\fR begins with
\fB@\fR, the remainder is a path to a file the token is read from (trailing
whitespace stripped), so the secret need not appear in \fBps\fR/argv.
.RE
\fB--os-insecure\fR
.RS 5
Skip TLS certificate verification for the OpenSearch endpoint.
.RE
\fB--os-datastream\fR
.RS 5
Target is an OpenSearch data stream; use the \fBcreate\fR bulk action instead of
\fBindex\fR.
.RE
\fB--otlp-url\fR \fIurl\fR
.RS 5
Export documents as OTLP/JSON to an OpenTelemetry endpoint (an OpenTelemetry
Collector, Grafana Alloy, or any OTLP/HTTP receiver): logs are POSTed to
\fIurl\fR\fB/v1/logs\fR and, with \fB--spans\fR, spans to \fIurl\fR\fB/v1/traces\fR,
with \fBContent-Type: application/json\fR. This defines the destination named
\fBdefault\fR; further destinations are declared in the configuration file (see
\fBMULTIPLE DESTINATIONS\fR).
The nested \fBresource\fR/\fBattributes\fR
objects are re-encoded into the strict OTLP \fBresourceLogs\fR/\fBresourceSpans\fR
envelope with typed \fBKeyValue\fR attributes. Batched per flush on a dedicated
output thread with retry/backoff; with \fB--cache-dir\fR a body that still fails
is written to disk and retried later (see \fB--cache-dir\fR), otherwise it is
dropped. Requires that \fBxrdmoncollect\fR was built with libcurl.
Pointing \fIurl\fR at Loki's OTLP endpoint (e.g. \fBhttp://loki:3100/otlp\fR)
feeds the ready-to-import \fBgrafana-loki-dashboard.json\fR; see the README.
.RE
\fB--otlp-token\fR \fItoken\fR
.RS 5
Bearer token for the OTLP endpoint, sent as an \fBAuthorization: Bearer \fR\fItoken\fR
header on every export (many collectors, e.g. Grafana Alloy or a gateway
Collector, gate ingestion on it). If \fItoken\fR begins with \fB@\fR, the remainder
is a path to a file the token is read from (trailing whitespace stripped).
.RE
\fB--otlp-insecure\fR
.RS 5
Skip TLS certificate verification for the OTLP endpoint.
.RE
\fB--cache-dir\fR \fIdir\fR
.RS 5
Directory for the on-failure disk cache, shared by the OpenSearch and OTLP sinks
(and, in shoveler mode, the frame spool). When set, a body that still fails to
POST after retries is written under \fIdir\fR
and retried oldest-first once the sink recovers; files left by a previous run are
replayed on startup. Each destination caches separately, since each recovers on its own schedule.
For the destination named \fBdefault\fR the OpenSearch \fB_bulk\fR bodies live
directly under \fIdir\fR and the OTLP bodies under the \fBotlp-logs\fR and
\fBotlp-traces\fR subdirectories (logs and traces replay to different
endpoints); a destination named \fIn\fR uses \fBos-\fR\fIn\fR, or
\fBotlp-\fR\fIn\fR\fB-logs\fR and \fBotlp-\fR\fIn\fR\fB-traces\fR. Shoveled
frame buffers live under \fBshovel\fR. The sink caches have no size limit (the
shovel spool is capped by \fB--spool-max\fR).
A destination that has a cache does not retry a live POST: it fails fast and
spills to disk, and the replay path carries the retry ladder. Holding a body for
the length of the ladder would let the queue behind it overflow with a working
cache configured.
When unset (the default), a body that cannot be posted is dropped (counted in
\fBxrootd_collector_dropped_bulk_total\fR / \fBxrootd_collector_otlp_dropped_total\fR).
.RE
\fB--forward\fR \fIhost:port\fR
.RS 5
Also stream each document as NDJSON over a TCP connection to \fIhost:port\fR
(for example a Logstash, Fluentd, or Vector buffering frontend). The connection
is re-established lazily after failures; documents produced while the consumer
is unreachable are dropped.
.RE
\fB--metrics-port\fR \fIport\fR
.RS 5
Serve aggregated metrics in Prometheus exposition format over HTTP on
\fIport\fR. A shoveler serves its own metric set under the
\fBxrootd_shoveler_\fR prefix.
.P
Every per-server series carries \fBcluster\fR and \fBserver\fR labels, so
\fBsum by (cluster)\fR gives a whole storage system. \fBcluster\fR is the
server's \fBall.sitename\fR, which this collector reads as the name of the
storage cluster (\fBeoscms\fR, \fBeosalice\fR) rather than the WLCG site: a
site holds several storage systems and nothing else on the monitoring wire
tells them apart. The WLCG site is not on the wire at all and comes from
\fB--site\fR (see below); without it there is no \fBsite\fR label. Both
cluster and server come from the \fB=\fR identity record and are provisional until it
arrives: a server not yet identified reads \fBcluster="unknown"\fR with its
numeric address as \fBserver\fR. That record defaults to hourly, so set
\fBident 300\fR in the server's \fBxrootd.monitor\fR directive to shorten
the window.
.P
Two ready-to-import Grafana dashboards for these metrics ship beside the
README: \fBgrafana-collector-dashboard.json\fR for the health of the
collector pipeline itself, and \fBgrafana-cluster-dashboard.json\fR for what
the monitored servers are doing. Neither carries per-user, per-VO or
per-dataset panels, because no metric here carries those labels; that detail
is in the documents.
.RE
\fB--site\fR \fIname\fR
.RS 5
Name the site this collector serves. The site is not carried on the monitoring
wire \(em \fBall.sitename\fR names the storage cluster, and one site holds
several \(em so it comes from here, on the deployment model of one collector
per site. Every metric series then carries a \fBsite\fR label, including the
collector's own unlabeled aggregates, and every document an \fBxrootd.site\fR
resource attribute. Unset by default, in which case nothing is tagged. A
collector receiving from several sites should instead derive the label per
server, e.g. with a Prometheus \fBmetric_relabel_configs\fR rule on
\fBserver\fR.
.RE
\fB--tcp-port\fR \fIport\fR
.RS 5
Also listen on TCP \fIport\fR for UDP monitoring datagrams encapsulated by
remote \fBxrdmoncollect\fR instances running in shoveler mode. Each received
datagram enters the normal pipeline attributed to its original source, so
correlation and packet-loss estimation are transport-agnostic. With
\fB--tcp-port\fR the UDP port becomes optional (a TCP-only central collector).
.RE
\fB--tcp-token\fR \fItoken\fR
.RS 5
Shared secret shovelers must present in the connection hello; compared in
constant time, with rejected connections counted in
\fBxrootd_collector_tcp_auth_failures_total\fR. If \fItoken\fR begins with
\fB@\fR, the remainder is a path to a file the token is read from. When unset,
any well-formed hello is accepted.
.RE
\fB--shovel\fR \fIhost:port\fR
.RS 5
Shoveler mode: relay every received UDP datagram over TCP to the central
collector's \fB--tcp-port\fR at \fIhost:port\fR instead of decoding it. No
correlation or document sink is active; options that require decoding are
warned about and ignored, so shovelers and the central collector can share one
configuration file. Cannot be combined with \fB--tcp-port\fR.
.RE
\fB--shovel-token\fR \fItoken\fR
.RS 5
Shared secret sent in the shovel connection hello; \fB@\fR\fIfile\fR reads it
from a file. An explicit rejection by the collector (token or protocol-version
mismatch) backs off for 30 seconds; plain network failures retry after 5.
.RE
\fB--spool-max\fR \fIsz\fR
.RS 5
Cap the shoveler's disk spool (under \fI--cache-dir\fR\fB/shovel\fR) at
approximately \fIsz\fR bytes, accepting a \fBK\fR/\fBM\fR/\fBG\fR suffix
(default \fB1G\fR; \fB0\fR = unbounded). When a new buffer would exceed the
cap, the \fIoldest\fR spooled buffers are evicted first (counted in
\fBxrootd_shoveler_spool_dropped_total\fR): during a long outage fresh
monitoring data is worth more than day-old data, and replay latency stays
bounded.
.RE
\fB--flush-count\fR \fIn\fR
.RS 5
Number of packets the receiver buffers into one batch before handing it to the
serializer; each batch becomes one OpenSearch \fB_bulk\fR POST (default: 500).
.RE
\fB--flush-secs\fR \fIn\fR
.RS 5
Hand off a partial batch after \fIn\fR seconds, bounding latency when packets
arrive slowly (default: 5).
.RE
\fB--rcvbuf\fR \fIsz\fR
.RS 5
Kernel UDP receive buffer size (\fBSO_RCVBUF\fR; K/M/G suffix; default 16M).
A larger buffer absorbs bursts and the brief windows when the receiver waits on
a full pipeline, so the kernel does not drop datagrams.
.RE
\fB--queue-depth\fR \fIn\fR
.RS 5
Number of packet batches that may be in flight between the receiver and the
serializer threads (default: 64). When the serializer falls behind, the receiver
waits for a free batch (backpressure) rather than dropping packets.
.RE
\fB--max-memory\fR \fIsz\fR
.RS 5
Cap the \fItotal resident memory\fR of the collector process at approximately
\fIsz\fR bytes, accepting a \fBK\fR/\fBM\fR/\fBG\fR suffix (default \fB1G\fR;
\fB0\fR = unbounded).
.P
The collector meets the cap by evicting correlation state. Once per control tick
it reads its own resident set size and, if that is over the cap, lowers the
budget it allows the per-server dictionaries and the open-file table to occupy;
the \fIleast-recently-used\fR entries go first, so a long-running transfer kept
warm by its in-flight \fIf\fR-stream snapshots is retained while cold, stranded
entries (for example an open whose close was lost to a dropped datagram) are
dropped. When resident memory falls back below the cap the budget is restored
gradually.
.P
This is \fIbest-effort, not a guarantee\fR. Only the correlation state is under
the collector's control: the receive queue, the pending output bodies and memory
the allocator has freed but not returned to the operating system are not. The
budget therefore has a floor below which correlation would stop working, and a
process that is over its cap with the budget already at that floor is reported
through \fBxrootd_collector_memory_floored_total\fR and a log message rather than
pursued until no state remains. The only hard cap is the one the kernel
enforces; see \fBMemoryMax=\fR in \fBsystemd.resource-control\fR(5).
.P
\fBxrootd_process_resident_memory_bytes\fR reports what is being capped,
\fBxrootd_collector_state_budget_bytes\fR the setpoint the loop is currently
enforcing, and \fBxrootd_collector_state_entries\fR how much correlation state
that is buying.
.P
\fBNote:\fR this option previously bounded an \fIestimate\fR of the correlation
state alone, and that estimate ran four to eight times below the memory really
used; its default was \fB256M\fR. A configuration carried over from that release
caps far less than its author intended \(em review it on upgrade.
.RE
\fB--max-entries\fR \fIn\fR
.RS 5
Optional hard cap on the total number of correlation entries retained
(\fB0\fR = off, the default). A deterministic backstop, independent of
\fB--max-memory\fR: it bounds a count rather than a quantity of memory, and
applies whether or not the memory cap is in use. Eviction is likewise
least-recently-used.
.RE
\fB--server-ttl\fR \fIs\fR
.RS 5
Reclaim a whole server incarnation (its dictionaries and \fIg\fR-stream counter
baselines) once it has reported nothing for more than \fIs\fR seconds, bounding
the accumulation of dead incarnations across server restarts and rolling
upgrades (default \fB86400\fR; \fB0\fR = never).
.RE
\fB--file-ttl\fR \fIs\fR
.RS 5
Expire an open-file entry that has not been touched for more than \fIs\fR
seconds (\fB0\fR = off, the default), counting it in
\fBstale_opens_total{cluster,server}\fR. Such an entry is a leaked open whose
close record was lost, most often to UDP loss; without a TTL it survives until
the client's disconnect sweep, or until the memory budget evicts it.
.sp
Applied only to servers that report in-flight transfer snapshots
(\fBxfr\fR on \fBxrootd.monitor fstat\fR), because only there does a live
transfer refresh its entry every reporting interval — anything older is
genuinely stranded. Set it to at least three times that server's \fBxfr\fR
period, or long-running transfers will be expired out from under their own
close records and every one of them will be reported as an orphan close.
.RE
\fB--state-file\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 5
Persist the correlation state across restarts: on shutdown (SIGTERM/SIGINT)
the whole state — the per-server dictionaries and open-file tables, the
\fIg\fR-stream counter baselines, and the decode statistics — is written to
\fIfile\fR as a single versioned JSON document (atomically, via a \fB.tmp\fR
rename), and on the next start it is reloaded before the first packet is
decoded, so a clean restart does not open a blind window while the
dictionaries repopulate. A snapshot is good for exactly one restart (the file
is removed once read) and is discarded when its format version does not match
or it is older than \fB--state-ttl\fR.
.sp
Under systemd the option defaults to
\fB$STATE_DIRECTORY/xrdmoncollect-state.json\fR (the bundled unit sets
\fBStateDirectory=xrootd\fR, so \fB/var/lib/xrootd/xrdmoncollect-state.json\fR);
outside systemd it is off unless given. An explicitly empty value disables
persistence.
.RE
\fB--state-ttl\fR \fIdur\fR
.RS 5
Discard a state snapshot older than \fIdur\fR on reload, accepting an
\fBs\fR/\fBm\fR/\fBh\fR/\fBd\fR suffix (default \fB15m\fR). After that long a
stop most correlation entries would be stale anyway (opens have closed, the
servers may have restarted with new dictids), so the collector starts fresh
instead.
.RE
\fB--scitags\fR \fIsrc\fR
.RS 5
Load a SciTags registry (the scitags.org schema: a top-level \fBexperiments\fR
array of \fB{expId, expName, activities:[{activityId, activityName}]}\fR). The
numeric SciTags experiment/activity ids carried on the \fIU\fR stream are then
additionally mapped to their names (\fBscitags.experiment\fR,
\fBscitags.activity\fR). The names stand on their own and are deliberately
\fInot\fR folded into \fBxrootd.vo\fR, which carries only a genuine VO. The
numeric ids are emitted either way; a missing or unparseable source is warned about and
otherwise ignored.
.sp
\fIsrc\fR is a local file path or an \fBhttp(s)://\fR URL (e.g.
\fBhttps://www.scitags.org/api.json\fR). A URL source requires that the
collector was built with libcurl.
.RE
\fB--scitags-refresh\fR \fIseconds\fR
.RS 5
Re-fetch a URL \fB--scitags\fR registry every \fIseconds\fR (default 3600; 0
disables) so a long-running collector tracks the published registry. The swap
is atomic with respect to the decode loop, and a failed re-fetch keeps the
current registry. Ignored for a file source.
.RE
\fB--dataset\fR \fIre\fR
.RS 5
Emit an \fBxrootd.dataset\fR attribute on every document that carries a
\fBfile.path\fR: \fIre\fR is a POSIX extended regular expression matched
against the path, and its first capture group becomes the value. For a
CMS-style namespace
(\fI/store/<class>/<era>/<primary-dataset>/...\fR) that is
.sp
.RS 3
\fB--dataset '^/store/[^/]+/[^/]+/([^/]+)/'\fR
.RE
.sp
Pick the group to suit the namespace: an EOS instance might capture the
project directory, ATLAS the dataset container. Dataset-level popularity
panels need this; nothing else does. The pattern is compiled once at start-up
(an uncompilable one is rejected) and matched once per emitted document on the
serializer thread, never in the receive path, so the cost is negligible at
realistic close rates \(em keep it anchored and simple all the same. A path it
does not match simply gets no attribute.
.RE
\fB--no-resolve\fR
.RS 5
Do not substitute the collector's local fully-qualified hostname for a server
that reports from the loopback address (\fB::1\fR/\fB127.0.0.1\fR, the common
co-located setup). With this flag \fBserver.address\fR keeps the numeric address
unless a hostname arrives on the \fI=\fR ident stream. Remote servers are never
reverse-resolved; their hostname comes from the \fI=\fR ident.
.RE
\fB--sessions\fR
.RS 5
Correlate per-session activity and emit a \fIsession\fR document on each client
disconnect (off by default). When disabled, no per-session rollup is accumulated
and no session document is produced. See the \fBDESCRIPTION\fR.
.RE
\fB--spans\fR
.RS 5
Also emit OpenTelemetry \fIspan\fR documents alongside the log records: a
file-operation span per close or failed operation, and a session span (the trace
root) per disconnect. The span reuses the log's \fBresource\fR, \fBattributes\fR
and \fBtraceId\fR/\fBspanId\fR but carries the span fields (\fBname\fR, \fBkind\fR,
\fBstartTimeUnixNano\fR/\fBendTimeUnixNano\fR, \fBstatus\fR), so a tracing backend
reconstructs the client session (login \(-> per-file operation). Off by default;
like the logs it can be high volume. The session span is only meaningful together
with \fB--sessions\fR.
.IP
Spans are what create traces: without \fB--spans\fR nothing is sent to
\fB/v1/traces\fR, so a logs-only OTLP export (however many \fBtraceId\fRs the logs
carry) shows no traces in Tempo/Grafana.
.RE
\fB--traces\fR
.RS 5
Emit a document per \fIt\fR-stream I/O record (read, write, open, close, readv,
disconnect). This is high volume. With \fB--spans\fR, each read/write/readv also
becomes a child \fIspan\fR nested under the file's transfer span, so the trace
waterfall reads session \(-> file \(-> I/O.
.RE
\fB--gstream\fR
.RS 5
Emit a document per \fIg\fR-stream (plugin) record.
.RE
\fB--redirects\fR
.RS 5
Emit a concluded-operation document per \fIr\fR-stream redirect record
(\fBxrootd.operation.state\fR \fBRedirected\fR, with the destination in the
\fBxrootd.redirect.*\fR attributes). Emitted mainly by redirectors/managers.
Redirects are counted
in the aggregated metrics regardless of this flag.
.RE
\fB--debug\fR
.RS 5
Also emit one JSON object per decoded record (raw, uncorrelated).
.RE
\fB-v\fR
.RS 5
Print decoder statistics on exit.
.RE
\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
.RS 5
Print the option summary and exit.
.RE
.SH SERVER CONFIGURATION
The richness of the transfer document depends on the server's
\fBxrootd.monitor\fR directive. File-level fields (\fBfile.path\fR, byte counts,
operation counts, and squared sums) require \fBfstat\fR with \fBlfn ops ssq
xfr\fR; identity fields such as \fBxrootd.auth.method\fR and the auth-derived VO
require the \fBauth\fR option. A representative configuration is:
.P
.RS 5
.nf
xrootd.monitor all flush 30s ident 5m fstat 60s lfn ops ssq xfr 1 \\
  auth dest fstat info user io <collector-host>:9930
.fi
.RE
.P
When \fBauth\fR is omitted, \fBxrootd.auth.method\fR and the auth-derived VO are
absent, but client version, IP version, and the remaining fields still
populate.
.P
The \fBxrootd.client.site\fR field is populated when the connecting client
advertises a site of its own: a client with \fBXRDSITE\fR (or \fBXRD_SITE\fR,
which takes precedence) set in its environment sends it at login, and the server
forwards it on the \fIu\fR-stream. This is independent both of the reporting
server's own \fBservice.namespace\fR and of the collector's \fB--site\fR.
.SS Tuning for pipeline resilience
The monitor streams are UDP: a datagram larger than the path MTU is fragmented
at the IP layer and the loss of any one fragment drops the whole datagram.
Keep \fIevery\fR stream buffer at or below the MTU: \fBmbuff\fR (trace),
\fBfbsz\fR (fstat, \fBdefault 65472\fR), \fBrbuff\fR (redirect, default 32K)
and \fBgbuff\fR (g-stream, default 32K) — 1472 for a 1500-byte IPv4 path, 1452
for IPv6. Note that before XRootD 6.1 \fBfbsz\fR does \fInot\fR follow
\fBmbuff\fR: setting \fBmbuff 1472\fR alone still emits ~64K fstat datagrams
(~44 fragments each), which is the stream the transfer documents are built
from — set \fBfbsz\fR explicitly on such servers. Do not go far below the MTU
either: a single fstat record (a close with a long LFN and an error message)
must fit the buffer or the server drops it.
.P
\fBflush\fR/\fBfstat\fR/\fBwindow\fR bound latency, not loss: smaller buffers
just flush more often. In the collector's metrics,
\fBpackets_lost_total{cluster,server,stream}\fR reports pseq gaps per stream class
(\fBmain\fR, \fBf\fR, \fBg:<provider>\fR — the server runs independent
sequence counters for the fstat stream and each g-stream provider); loss
concentrated on \fBf\fR while \fBt\fR arrives fine is the fragmentation
signature, loss on all streams points at the network or the collector's
receive buffer. \fBpackets_total{cluster,server,stream}\fR carries the same
\fB{server,stream}\fR labels, so a per-source loss percentage is
\fBpackets_lost_total / packets_total\fR.
\fBmalformed_total{server,stream,reason}\fR breaks out
structurally invalid packets (\fBbad_plen\fR, \fBshort_packet\fR,
\fBbad_record\fR, \fBtrailing_bytes\fR, \fBtruncated_string\fR); run with
\fB--debug\fR to log the server, stream, and reason of each rejected packet.
\fBunknown_packets_total\fR counts unhandled stream codes (usually stray
traffic on the port).
\fBinvalid_utf8_total\fR counts strings arriving off the wire whose bytes were
not valid UTF-8. Each offending byte is replaced with U+FFFD as the string is
read, before any field is cut out of it, so the document is emitted with one
mangled field rather than dropped and the state file and metric labels carry
the same repaired bytes. A steady rate means files are being named in an
encoding other than UTF-8, which JSON cannot represent faithfully.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Options may be supplied in an INI configuration file as an alternative to the
command line. The file is loaded from the path given with \fB-c\fR, or
automatically from \fI/etc/xrootd/xrdmoncollect.cfg\fR when present. All keys are
placed in a single \fB[xrdmoncollect]\fR section and use the long-option names
(for example \fBos-url\fR, \fBforward\fR, \fBmax-memory\fR); command-line
options override values from the file. Four keys are spelled differently from
the option they set, because those options have only a short form:
\fBport\fR (\fB-p\fR, also accepted as \fBudp-port\fR), \fBbind\fR (\fB-b\fR),
\fBoutput\fR (\fB-o\fR) and \fBverbose\fR (\fB-v\fR). Flags take a boolean
value, e.g. \fBspans = true\fR. For example:
.P
.RS 5
.nf
[xrdmoncollect]
port = 9930
os-url = https://opensearch.example.org:9200
forward = logstash.example.org:5044
max-memory = 1G
.fi
.RE
.SS Multiple destinations
Both HTTP sinks accept any number of destinations. The flat \fBos-*\fR and
\fBotlp-*\fR keys, and their command-line equivalents, define the one named
\fBdefault\fR; each additional endpoint gets an \fB[opensearch "\fIname\fB"]\fR
or \fB[otlp "\fIname\fB"]\fR section. The typical use is a site keeping its own
monitoring while also feeding a central collector, from one instance rather than
two. There is no command-line equivalent.
.P
.RS 5
.nf
[xrdmoncollect]
port = 9930
cache-dir = /var/lib/xrootd/moncollect

[otlp "local"]
url = http://alloy.example.org:4318

[otlp "wlcg"]
url = https://otel.wlcg.example:4318
token = @/etc/xrootd/wlcg.token

[opensearch "site"]
url = https://opensearch.example.org:9200
index = site-transfers
.fi
.RE
.P
Keys inside an \fB[opensearch]\fR section are \fBurl\fR (required), \fBindex\fR,
\fBuser\fR, \fBpass\fR, \fBtoken\fR, \fBinsecure\fR and \fBdatastream\fR; inside
an \fB[otlp]\fR section, \fBurl\fR (required), \fBtoken\fR and \fBinsecure\fR.
They are the long-option names without the \fBos-\fR/\fBotlp-\fR prefix, and an
unrecognised one is a fatal error.
.P
A named section \fBinherits nothing\fR from the flat keys, not even the index:
inheriting would make the result depend on whether a value came from the file or
the command line, since the two are read in that order, and an inherited
credential would silently be POSTed to an endpoint it was never meant for. Every
key defaults to what the corresponding option defaults to.
.P
Every destination runs its own thread, queue, retry state and disk-cache
directory, and every sink metric carries a \fBdestination\fR label. One that is
slow or unreachable can neither stall the collector nor affect another: its
queue is bounded in bytes and drops its oldest body when full, counted in
\fBxrootd_collector_post_overflow_total\fR /
\fBxrootd_collector_otlp_overflow_total\fR (which is distinct from a drop after
a failed POST -- an overflow means the destination cannot keep up, not that a
disk cache is missing).
.P
A section name must start with a letter or digit and may contain only letters,
digits, \fB_\fR, \fB-\fR and \fB.\fR; it becomes both a directory component
under \fB--cache-dir\fR and a Prometheus label value. Names are compared without
regard to case, and \fBdefault\fR is reserved for the flat keys.
.SS Document filtering
The configuration file may also carry any number of \fB[filter "\fIname\fB"]\fR
sections, each one a rule matched against every finished document just before it
reaches the output sinks. A matching rule either suppresses the document or tags
it with a label, so activity that is uninteresting to end users (a storage
instance's own internal traffic, say) can be dropped at the collector or merely
marked for filtering downstream. There is no command-line equivalent.
.P
.RS 5
.nf
[filter "eos-internal"]
user     = daemon, root, ~^[0-9]+$
authprot = sss
action   = tag
label    = internal

[filter "eos-proc"]
path   = /eos/*/proc/*
action = drop
.fi
.RE
.P
Within a rule, \fBaction\fR (\fBtag\fR, the default, \fBdrop\fR or \fBkeep\fR)
and \fBlabel\fR (default: the rule name) are reserved; every other key is a
match condition, and an unrecognised one is a start-up error. Conditions are
ANDed. A value is a comma-separated OR-list whose alternatives are matched as an
exact string, as a glob when they contain \fB*\fR or \fB?\fR, or as a POSIX
extended regular expression when they begin with \fB~\fR; a leading \fB!\fR
negates the condition. A condition naming a field the document does not carry
never matches, negated or not.
.P
Rules are evaluated in no particular order: every matching rule contributes its
label to the \fBxrootd.filter.labels\fR attribute, and the document is dropped
only when some matching rule says \fBdrop\fR and none says \fBkeep\fR.
.P
Condition keys are \fBuser\fR, \fBuserid\fR, \fBrole\fR, \fBvo\fR, \fBgroups\fR,
\fBauthprot\fR, \fBproto\fR, \fBscheme\fR, \fBclient\fR, \fBclientip\fR,
\fBclientsite\fR, \fBapp\fR, \fBappver\fR, \fBappinfo\fR, \fBappid\fR,
\fBexperiment\fR, \fBactivity\fR, \fBpath\fR, \fBdir\fR, \fBfilename\fR,
\fBext\fR, \fBdataset\fR, \fBevent\fR, \fBop\fR, \fBstate\fR,
\fBerror\fR, \fBprovider\fR, \fBtarget\fR, \fBsession\fR, \fBseverity\fR,
\fBserver\fR, \fBsite\fR, \fBcluster\fR, \fBservice\fR, \fBprogram\fR
and \fBversion\fR, or a
raw \fBattributes.\fIkey\fR / \fBresource.\fIkey\fR path for any other field.
.P
Filtering applies to the emitted documents only: a dropped document is still
counted in the I/O, session and error series and still folded into its session
rollup, so the aggregate view of the cluster remains complete. The exception is
\fBdocuments_total\fR, which counts what was actually emitted. The number
suppressed is exported as \fBfiltered_documents_total\fR and reported as
\fBfiltered=\fR in the \fB-v\fR exit summary.
.P
Note that a filter key must start in column 1: an indented line is appended to
the preceding key's value by the INI parser's multi-line continuation, which
would fold the rest of the rule into its first key.
.SH SYSTEMD INTEGRATION
The bundled \fBxrdmoncollect.service\fR unit runs the collector as
\fBType=notify\fR: the daemon signals readiness once its listeners and worker
threads are up, and signals \fBSTOPPING=1\fR when shutdown begins.
.P
For zero-loss restarts, enable the companion socket unit:
.P
.RS 5
.nf
systemctl enable --now xrdmoncollect.socket
.fi
.RE
.P
With socket activation, systemd creates and \fIholds\fR the listening
sockets (\fBListenDatagram=\fR for the UDP monitor port, optionally
\fBListenStream=\fR for the TCP shovel listener) and passes them to the
collector, which matches them against its configured ports by socket type and
port number. Because the sockets never close across a service restart —
whether a clean \fBsystemctl restart\fR or a crash with \fBRestart=on-failure\fR
— datagrams keep queueing in the kernel receive buffer (sized with the socket
unit's \fBReceiveBuffer=\fR, applied by systemd as root and therefore not
capped by \fBnet.core.rmem_max\fR) and shovel connections queue in the listen
backlog. Combined with \fB--state-file\fR, a collector restart is invisible to
the monitoring pipeline as long as the downtime fits the receive buffer.
.P
The ports in the socket unit must match \fBudp-port\fR/\fBtcp-port\fR in the
configuration file; when no inherited socket fits a configured port the
collector binds that socket itself, so a mismatch merely loses the zero-loss
property. Without the socket unit (or outside systemd) nothing changes: the
collector binds its sockets as usual.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
\fBSTATE_DIRECTORY\fR
.RS 5
Set by systemd from \fBStateDirectory=\fR. When present and \fB--state-file\fR
was not given, the correlation snapshot defaults to
\fB$STATE_DIRECTORY/xrdmoncollect-state.json\fR.
.RE
\fBLISTEN_FDS\fR, \fBLISTEN_PID\fR
.RS 5
Set by systemd socket activation. Sockets passed this way are adopted in
preference to binding; see \fBSYSTEMD INTEGRATION\fR.
.RE
\fBNOTIFY_SOCKET\fR
.RS 5
Set by systemd for \fBType=notify\fR. Used to report readiness and, on
shutdown, that the collector is stopping. Only when built with libsystemd.
.RE
\fBMALLOC_ARENA_MAX\fR, \fBGLIBC_TUNABLES\fR, \fBMALLOC_MMAP_THRESHOLD_\fR, \fBMALLOC_TRIM_THRESHOLD_\fR
.RS 5
The collector tunes the glibc allocator at start-up \(em bounding arenas,
pinning the mmap threshold so large output bodies are returned to the operating
system on free, and lowering the trim threshold \(em because the default
behaviour makes resident memory a poor proxy for memory in use, which is what
\fB--max-memory\fR steers on. Any of these set in the environment is left
alone, so the tuning can be overridden without rebuilding.
.RE
.SH FILES
\fI/etc/xrootd/xrdmoncollect.cfg\fR
.RS 5
Default configuration file, used when it exists and \fB-c\fR was not given.
.RE
\fI$STATE_DIRECTORY/xrdmoncollect-state.json\fR
.RS 5
Correlation snapshot; see \fB--state-file\fR. Written at shutdown and removed
once read.
.RE
\fI<cache-dir>\fR
.RS 5
On-failure spool; see \fB--cache-dir\fR. The destination named \fBdefault\fR
uses the directory itself for OpenSearch and \fIotlp-logs\fR/\fIotlp-traces\fR
beneath it; every other destination uses \fIos-<name>\fR and
\fIotlp-<name>-logs\fR/\fIotlp-<name>-traces\fR.
.RE
.SH NOTES
Documentation for all components associated with \fBxrdmoncollect\fR can be
found at https://xrootd.org/docs.html
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors yield an error message and a non-zero exit status. The program otherwise
runs until terminated; use the kill command to stop it.
